4. Discussion
4.1. Theeffectofaudioandvisualindicatorsonsoundscapeperception
Table 6 illustrates the correlation between the sound source and
visual elements and soundscape emotions and cognition. For a more
meaningful interpretation, we examined the relationship that demon-
strates the value of the correlation coefficient above the small effect
(0.2) of Cohen’s convention of a Pearson correlation (Cohen, West, &
Aiken,2014).First,itwasfoundtohaveapositiveeffectontheeva-
luation of urban parks (p < 0.01); with a value of 0.56, the sound
sourceindicatordemonstratedthatbirdsonghasastrongpositivecor-
relation with tranquil/peaceful and comfort, and a strong negative
correlation with confusing/unpleasant and perceived loudness, with
values of −0.71 and −0.65, respectively. Similar to birdsong, the
water sound also had a positive effect on the evaluation of a park’s
calmness( =0.29,p < 0.05),indicatingthatnaturalsoundsmadea
positivecontributiontothesoundscapeevaluationofurbanparks(De
Coensel, Vanwetswinkel, & Botteldooren, 2011). For human sound,
there was a strong positive relationship of 0.68 with the dynamic/li-
velinessoftheparkandaveryhighpositivecorrelationof0.79withthe
perceivedcrowddensity.Contrarytonaturalsound,thehumansound
had a negative relationship with a park’s tranquil/peaceful
( =−0.69,p < 0.01)andcomfort( =−0.28,p < 0.05)levels,
and a positive relationship with perceived loudness ( = 0.67,
p < 0.01)andconfusing/unpleasant( =0.30,p < 0.05).Therefore,
it was confirmed that human sound plays an important role in de-
terminingemotionsandcognitionrelatedtothecalmnessorliveliness
of park soundscapes (Axelsson, 2015; Axelsson, Nilsson, Hellström, &
Lundén,2014).
Looking atthe relationship betweensound sources and visualele-
ments, traffic noise had a high positive correlation with vehicles
(γ = 0.65, p < 0.01), people (γ = 0.60, p < 0.01), and roads
(γ=0.56,p < 0.01).Humansoundalsohadahighcorrelationwith
people(γ=0.80,p < 0.01),vehicles(γ=0.50,p < 0.01),androads
(γ = 0.41, p < 0.01), from which it is possible to determine that
humanactivitiesthatcausedsoundoccurredneartheroads.Amongthe
natural sounds, birdsongs had a positive correlation with greenery
(γ=0.68,p < 0.01)andanegativecorrelationwithvehicles(γ=-
0.43,p < 0.01),whilewatersoundsonlydemonstratedahighpositive
correlation with water spaces (γ = 0.75,p < 0.01). One interesting
fact was found in the relationship between changes in the audio and
visual indicator identification rate and changes in emotions and cog-
nitionovertime.Thechangeinbirdsongwaspositivelyrelatedtothe
amount of confusing/unpleasant change. Although birdsong can be
usedtoinfluencethecomplexanduncomfortablesoundenvironmentof
a park, randomly increasing birdsong over time can have a negative
impactonapark’ssoundenvironment.
Next, for visual elements, vehicles tended to be similar to traffic
sounds, people were similar to humans, and birdsong was similar to
greenery. Many visual indicators had a positive correlation with dy-
namic/lively,andavisualidentificationofpeoplewasthemostcritical
factor ( = 0.72, p < 0.01). Roads, open spaces, and sky also had
positivecorrelationswithvaluesof0.36,0.48,and0.50,respectively.
Thiscanbeexplainedbythefactthattheserepresentedplaceswhere
people’s activities occur in the park. In the case of the sky, the value
increasedwiththeincreaseinopenspaces.
